1
00:00:00,110 --> 00:00:02,980
人工智能怎么知道煮鸡蛋要花多长时间？
How does an AI know how long to boil an egg?

2
00:00:03,694 --> 00:00:06,420
它从未煮过蛋，也没感到过饥饿。
It has never cooked one, and it has never been hungry.

3
00:00:07,598 --> 00:00:09,140
它的内部也没有食谱。
There's no cookbook inside it either.

4
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:14,960
打开其中一个大型模型，你会发现里面只有数字，数量多到和银河系中的恒星数量差不多。
Open up one of the big ones, and you'll find only numbers, about as many as our galaxy has stars.

5
00:00:16,280 --> 00:00:20,020
所以今天，我们将深入这些数字中去寻找你问题的答案。
So today, we're going to follow your question in among those numbers.

6
00:00:20,680 --> 00:00:26,100
我们将观察它的词汇如何变成地图上的位置，在那里，鸡蛋就在牛奶旁边。
We'll watch its words turn into places on a map where egg lives next door to milk.

7
00:00:26,638 --> 00:00:38,570
我们将深入到单个数字，那是覆盖曼哈顿三分之二面积的马赛克中的一块瓷砖，
然后我们又会爬回到它被创造者查看内部并发现它在提前规划的那一天之后，
We'll go down to a single number, one tile of a mosaic that would cover two-thirds of Manhattan,
and will climb back out past the day its own makers looked inside and caught it planning ahead,

8
00:00:38,570 --> 00:00:38,940
我们将深入到单个数字，那是覆盖曼哈顿三分之二面积的马赛克中的一块瓷砖，
然后我们又会爬回到它被创造者查看内部并发现它在提前规划的那一天之后，
all the way to a gold medal score in mathematics.

9
00:00:38,940 --> 00:00:41,620
一直追溯到数学领域的金牌水平。
all the way to a gold medal score in mathematics.

10
00:00:42,702 --> 00:00:47,076
那么这些毫无意义的数字，一个接一个地，是如何学会关于鸡蛋知识的？
So how do numbers that mean nothing, one by one, come to know about eggs?

11
00:00:52,686 --> 00:00:54,510
从你亲眼见过的东西开始。
Start with something you have seen for yourself.

12
00:00:55,278 --> 00:01:01,790
当 ChatGPT 或 Claude 回答你时，词语一个接一个地出现，就像有人在打字一样。
When ChatGPT or Claude answers you, the words arrive one after another, as if somebody were typing.

13
00:01:02,638 --> 00:01:03,530
这并不是为了做秀。
It isn't for show.

14
00:01:04,269 --> 00:01:09,210
你正在目睹答案的生成过程，因为屏幕后面并没有预先准备好的成品答案。
You are watching the answer being made because there is no finished answer waiting behind the screen.

15
00:01:09,742 --> 00:01:13,890
模型根据目前已知的所有信息，产出的仅仅是下一个词。
All the model ever produces is the next word, given everything so far.

16
00:01:15,278 --> 00:01:18,550
所以它接收到你的问题：我该煮多久的蛋？
So it takes in your question, how long do I boil an egg?

17
00:01:18,730 --> 00:01:22,410
然后它给它所知道的每一个词片段都标注出出现的概率。
And it gives every piece of a word it knows its odds of coming next.

18
00:01:23,246 --> 00:01:32,484
我们无法查看那些著名的模型内部，所以我们将这个问题输入到一个基于相同设计的小型开源模
型中，其中三个片段包含了几乎所有的概率。
We can't see inside the famous ones, so we ran this question through a small open
model that is built on the same design and three pieces shared almost all of the odds.

19
00:01:33,050 --> 00:01:40,370
分别是 The、A 和 Bo，因为模型通过拼接词块来构建长词，而“煮”的开头就是这些。
The, A, and Bo, which is how boiling begins because the model builds longer words out of pieces.

20
00:01:41,310 --> 00:01:46,930
然后一个词块会根据概率被抽取出来，就像在转轮上抽奖一样，胜率最高的选项占据最大的扇区。
One piece is then drawn by chance, as if on a wheel where the favorite has the widest slice.

21
00:01:47,790 --> 00:01:55,108
这种随机性赋予了它的写作多样性，这也是为什么同一个问题问两次，
会用不同的措辞给出非常相似的建议。
The chance gives its writing variety and it is why the same question, asked twice, brings much the same advice in different words.

22
00:01:56,206 --> 00:02:02,850
在我们的测试中，转轮停在了 THE 上，而就在那一刻，答案中还没有任何其他词被写出来。
On our run, the wheel stopped on THE, and at that moment, not one more word of the answer had been written anywhere.

23
00:02:03,726 --> 00:02:09,389
THE 被添加到你问题的末尾，模型再次开始计算接下来应该是什么。
THE is added to the end of your question, and the model sets off again to work out what should follow it.

24
00:02:09,966 --> 00:02:15,290
我们将每一次这样的过程称为一次推理步，你看到的每一个出现的片段都是多了一次推理步。
We'll call each of those one trip through the model, and every piece you see arrive is one more trip.

25
00:02:16,334 --> 00:02:22,116
经过几百次推理步后，它告诉你任何厨师都会说的话：这取决于你喜欢什么样的蛋黄。
A couple hundred trips later, it has told you what any cook would, that it depends how you like your yolk.

26
00:02:23,182 --> 00:02:25,170
到那时，一切都取决于这些概率。
Everything hangs on those odds, then.

27
00:02:25,902 --> 00:02:30,590
为了看看这些概率从何而来，我们必须极大地放慢一次推理步的速度，并深入其中去观察。
To see where they come from, we have to slow one trip right down and ride through it.

28
00:02:33,100 --> 00:02:36,960
这段旅程始于我们第一段影片结束的地方，就在那个芯片的底部。
The ride begins where our first film stopped, at the bottom of that chip.

29
00:02:37,580 --> 00:02:40,000
我们在那里提到了某件事，但从未解释过它。
We said something there and never explained it.

30
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:49,200
那就是回答你的东西是一个非常长的数字列表，为了选择每一个词片段，
芯片会进行数十亿次的乘法运算。
That the thing that answers you is a very long list of numbers, and that to choose each piece of a word,
the chip multiplies its way through billions of them.

31
00:02:49,614 --> 00:02:55,540
ChatGPT、Claude 和 Gemini 背后的模型都将它们的列表锁在内部。
The models behind ChatGPT, Claude, and Gemini keep their lists locked away.

32
00:02:56,302 --> 00:03:03,800
但它们共同构建的架构是公开的，一种被称为
Transformer 的神经网络，也就是 ChatGPT 中的那个 T。
But the design they are all built on is public, a kind of neural network called a transformer, the T in ChatGPT.

33
00:03:04,366 --> 00:03:06,116
有些列表规模也非常庞大。
And so are some very large lists.

34
00:03:06,926 --> 00:03:10,880
其中最大的一个包含 4050 亿个数字。
One of the largest runs to 405 billion numbers.

35
00:03:11,694 --> 00:03:20,960
如果手动处理这个列表，每秒计算一次，正如我们第一部影片所算的，
仅仅处理一个词的一个片段就需要大约 13000 年。
Work through that list by hand, one multiplication a second, and a single piece
of a word would take you about 13,000 years, as our first film worked out.

36
00:03:21,710 --> 00:03:24,700
而要给出一个像“鸡蛋”这么长的答案，则需要超过两百万次运算。
An answer, as long as the eggs, would take more than two million.

37
00:03:25,140 --> 00:03:31,420
这比人类提出这个问题的时间还要长，也是德克萨斯州那些建筑之所以建得那么大的原因之一。
Longer than there have been people to ask the question, and one reason buildings like the one in Texas are the size they are.

38
00:03:32,494 --> 00:03:38,780
这些数字中的每一个都可以稍微调高或调低一点，一旦改变，模型就会产生不同的反应。
Each of those numbers can be set a little higher or a little lower, and the model does something different when it is.

39
00:03:39,560 --> 00:03:42,820
工程师称它们为参数或权重。
Engineers call them parameters or weights.

40
00:03:43,340 --> 00:03:44,720
我们称它们为“设置”。
We'll call them its settings.

41
00:03:45,934 --> 00:03:46,596
为什么叫权重？
Why weights?

42
00:03:47,406 --> 00:03:49,124
因为这个概念源自大脑。
Because the idea came from the brain.

43
00:03:49,980 --> 00:03:54,780
1943 年，两名科学家将脑细胞描述为一个微小的开关。
In 1943, two scientists described a brain cell as a tiny switch.

44
00:03:55,240 --> 00:03:59,500
它会累加周围细胞传来的信号，如果总和足够大，它就会发出信号。
It adds up the signals from the cells around it and fires if the total is big enough.

45
00:04:00,160 --> 00:04:04,460
1958 年，弗兰克·罗森布拉特证明了由这些单元组成的网络可以进行学习。
In 1958, Frank Rosenblatt showed that a network of them could learn.

46
00:04:04,940 --> 00:04:09,740
每当它猜错时，它就会增加或减少连接的强度。
Each time it guessed wrong, it turned the strengths of its connections up or down.

47
00:04:09,740 --> 00:04:11,980
这些强度就是权重。
Those strengths are the weights.

48
00:04:12,820 --> 00:04:17,000
将数百万个这样的微小求和堆叠成多层，就构成了神经网络。
Stack millions of these little sums in layers, and that's a neural network.

49
00:04:18,158 --> 00:04:19,588
这种相似性到此为止。
That's about where the likeness ends.

50
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:27,780
真实的脑细胞比一个简单的求和要复杂得多，而且
没有任何孩子是靠阅读数万亿个词才学会说话的。
A real brain cell is far more complicated than one little sum, and no child learns to talk by reading trillions of words.

51
00:04:28,720 --> 00:04:34,640
这些求和的数量如此庞大且高度相似，以至于现在的芯片专门配备了仅用于处理这类运算的部件。
These sums are so many, and so alike, that chips now carry parts built for little else.

52
00:04:34,640 --> 00:04:41,900
Apple 将其中一个称为神经网络引擎，并且在它最新的芯片中，
每个图形核心都配备了一个神经网络加速器。
Apple calls one its neural engine, and its newest chips put a neural accelerator in every graphics core.

53
00:04:42,510 --> 00:04:46,040
关于其内部运行机制的内容，将在另一部影片中专门介绍。
What happens inside one is coming in a film of its own.

54
00:04:48,590 --> 00:04:50,750
第一个问题是，芯片无法阅读。
The first problem is that the chip can't read.

55
00:04:51,502 --> 00:04:58,770
正如我们上次看到的，它的开关只能处理数字，所以首先，你的问题必须不再是文字。
As we saw last time, its switches can only work with numbers, so before anything else, your question has to stop being words.

56
00:04:59,950 --> 00:05:05,910
它被切成被称为“词元”的片段，大部分是完整的单词，每个片段都会被替换成一组数字。
It's cut into pieces called tokens, most of them whole words, and each piece is swapped for a list of numbers.

57
00:05:07,086 --> 00:05:08,690
这些数字就像一个地址。
Those numbers work like an address.

58
00:05:09,454 --> 00:05:19,090
给我两个数字，一个代表向北的距离，一个代表向东的距离，我就能在地图上找到任何城镇，
而那些拥有几乎相同数值的城镇就会成为邻居。
Give me two numbers, one for how far north and one for how far east, and I can find any town on a map,
and towns with nearly the same numbers will be neighbors.

59
00:05:20,302 --> 00:05:27,250
模型为每一个片段都分配了这样一个地址，在语义地图上，用法相似的词会聚集在一起。
The model gives every piece an address like that, on a map of meaning, where words that are used alike live close together.

60
00:05:27,970 --> 00:05:33,490
“鸡蛋”会待在“牛奶”、“鸡肉”和“奶酪”旁边，而离“因此”很远。
Egg lives beside milk, chicken and cheese, and a long way from therefore.

61
00:05:34,798 --> 00:05:45,970
然而两个数字显然远远不够，因为关于一个词的描述远不止于“向北”和“向东”，
比如它是不是食物、是否是活物、它是实物还是某种动作。
Two numbers wouldn't be nearly enough though, because there's far more to say about a word than north and east, whether it's food,
whether it's alive, whether it's a thing or something you do.

62
00:05:46,410 --> 00:05:49,730
因此，每个地址都对应成千上万个数字。
So each address runs to thousands of numbers.

63
00:05:50,606 --> 00:05:56,510
你没法想象一个拥有成千上万个方向的地图，别人也没法想象，但原理是一样的。
You can't picture a map with thousands of directions, and neither can anyone else, but the idea is the same.

64
00:05:56,930 --> 00:05:58,596
「近」意味着相似。
Near means alike.

65
00:05:59,770 --> 00:06:07,810
这张图是真实的，它取自一个小型的开源模型并被
压平以便我们观察，其中的方向都有特定的含义。
This map is a real one, taken from a small open model and squashed flat so that we can look at it, and its directions mean something.

66
00:06:08,630 --> 00:06:16,676
从“男人”跨到“女人”，再从“国王”跨过去，你落点位置最近的词就是“女王”。
Take the step that leads from man to woman, take it again from king, and the nearest other word to where you land is queen.

67
00:06:17,454 --> 00:06:21,988
没人画过这张地图，也没人告诉过模型“鸡蛋”和“牛奶”是相关的。
Nobody drew this map, and nobody ever told the model that eggs go with milk.

68
00:06:23,110 --> 00:06:26,660
模型在它的权重中保留了每一个词的地址。
The model keeps every piece's address among its settings.

69
00:06:27,534 --> 00:06:32,250
针对你的问题，它把其中八个连续提取出来，从现在起我们就遵循这一行。
For your question it copies eight of them out in a row and that row is what we follow from here.

70
00:06:33,038 --> 00:06:36,190
这些权重就像厨房，在它回答你的时候不会改变。
The settings are the kitchen and they don't change while it answers you.

71
00:06:36,990 --> 00:06:38,270
而这一行就是一道菜。
The row is the dish.

72
00:06:38,650 --> 00:06:42,950
每一步，它的数字都会与某些权重相乘，并产生变化。
At every step its numbers are multiplied by some of the settings and come out changed.

73
00:06:45,630 --> 00:06:50,350
现在烹饪开始了，它从一个你每天都在无意识中解决的问题开始。
Now the cooking starts and it starts with a problem you solve every day without noticing.

74
00:06:51,390 --> 00:06:55,230
“煮”可能是你对鸡蛋的操作，也可能指你脖子后方的肿块。
Boil could be what you do to an egg or a sore on the back of your neck.

75
00:06:55,990 --> 00:06:58,750
“蛋”可能是早餐，也可能是窝里的东西。
Egg could be breakfast or something in a nest.

76
00:06:59,566 --> 00:07:05,250
无论哪种情况，它的地址都是一样的，所以模型必须根据词语所在的上下文来判断你的意思。
The address is the same either way, so the model has to work out which one you meant from the company the word keeps.

77
00:07:06,382 --> 00:07:09,670
因此，每个词都会回顾它之前的词。
So every word looks back over the words that came before it.

78
00:07:10,390 --> 00:07:15,070
“煮”找到了“我”，所以它可能是一个动作，而不是肿块。
Boil finds do I, so it's probably something you do, and not a sore.

79
00:07:15,830 --> 00:07:19,170
“蛋”找到了“煮”，所以这个蛋很可能是早餐。
Egg finds boil, so this egg is probably breakfast.

80
00:07:19,886 --> 00:07:21,890
这种“寻找”是通过乘法完成的。
The looking is done by multiplying.

81
00:07:22,770 --> 00:07:30,070
一个词的数字与它之前每个词的数字进行对比，权重决定哪些信息重要，
然后将少量的相关信息混合进去。
A word's numbers are held up against those of each word before it, the
settings decide which them matter, and a little of those is mixed in.

82
00:07:30,734 --> 00:07:33,650
所以在这一行中，关于“蛋”的数值发生了偏移。
So in the row, the numbers for egg shift.

83
00:07:34,090 --> 00:07:37,570
它最初只是任何一个普通的“蛋”，现在变成了你问题中的那个“蛋”。
It started as any egg, and it is becoming the egg in your question.

84
00:07:38,350 --> 00:07:41,630
它在权重中的原始地址保持不变。
Its address, among the settings, stays as it was.

85
00:07:42,478 --> 00:07:45,370
这种回顾机制是 Transformer 的核心。
This looking back is the heart of the transformer.

86
00:07:46,090 --> 00:07:56,230
它被称为注意力机制，谷歌的八位研究员在 2017 年发布该设计时，
给他们的论文取名为《注意力就是你所需的一切》。
It is called Attention, and the eight researchers at Google who
published the design in 2017 named their paper attention is all you need.

87
00:07:57,350 --> 00:08:10,430
然后每个词都会独立地与更多的权重相乘，这时模型会融入它对世界的更多认知，
比如蛋是在水中煮的，而且是煮几分钟而不是几天。
Then each word, on its own, is multiplied through more of the settings, and this is where the model stirs in much of what it knows
about the world, that eggs are boiled in water, and for minutes rather than days.

88
00:08:11,598 --> 00:08:14,590
这就是一轮，而一轮往往无法解决太多问题。
That's one round, and one round can't settle much.

89
00:08:15,342 --> 00:08:22,050
这就像填字游戏的第一次尝试，有一半的线索因为依赖你还没填入的答案而无法破解。
It's like the first pass at a crossword, when half the clues can't be solved because they hang on answers you haven't filled in yet.

90
00:08:22,798 --> 00:08:28,550
当“蛋”回顾“煮”时，“煮”本身还没有在“平底锅”和“翱翔”之间做出决定。
When Egg looked back at Boil, Boil itself hadn't yet decided between the Pan and the Soar.

91
00:08:29,294 --> 00:08:33,690
所以你再次遍历整个网格，你填入的每一个答案都会帮助确定下一个。
So you go around the grid again and each answer you've penciled in helps with the next.

92
00:08:34,510 --> 00:08:43,549
模型进行第二轮处理，使用它自己的权重，接着是第三轮，每一次，
每个词都在观察那些已经做出更多决定的邻近词。
The model has a second round with settings of its own and then a third and each time
every word is looking back at neighbors that have made up their minds a little more.

93
00:08:44,302 --> 00:08:49,730
这个开放模型在单次推理步中包含 126 轮，也就是 126 层。
That same open model has a 126 rounds, or layers, in a single trip.

94
00:08:51,370 --> 00:08:52,490
为什么是 126？
Why 126?

95
00:08:52,850 --> 00:08:53,990
这没有固定公式。
There's no formula.

96
00:08:54,810 --> 00:09:02,930
它的开发者在尝试不同的规模，更多的轮数能让每个词都有
更多步骤的推导过程，而且多年来，规模越大效果就越好。
Its builders try sizes, more rounds give every word more steps of working out, and for years now, Bigger has kept on working better.

97
00:09:05,510 --> 00:09:08,490
在最后一轮之后，看这一行的最末端。
After the last round, look at the very end of the row.

98
00:09:09,250 --> 00:09:15,250
在你的问号后面有一个模型制造者放在那里的标记，它的意思实际上就是：现在该你回答了。
Behind your question mark sits a mark the model's makers put there, which says in effect, now you answer.

99
00:09:15,250 --> 00:09:26,170
这个最后的位置一直在回顾你提出的整个问题，经过一轮又一轮的推演，到目前为止，
它的数字也成了一种地址，即接下来应该出现的内容的地址。
That last place has been looking back over your whole question, round after round, and
by now its numbers are a kind of address too, the address of what ought to come next.

100
00:09:27,278 --> 00:09:29,350
这个地址必须再次变成一个词。
An address has to become a word again.

101
00:09:30,094 --> 00:09:39,730
于是模型将它与它为最后这一步保留的地址进行对比，每一个已知片段都有一个对应的地址，
并给每个片段计算概率，越接近就越好。
So the model holds it up against the addresses it keeps for this last step, one for every piece it knows,
and gives each piece its odds, the nearer the better.

102
00:09:39,730 --> 00:09:45,250
这些就是轮盘上的概率，这就是为什么它最终占据了最大的份额。
Those are the odds on the wheel and that's how the came to have the widest slice.

103
00:09:46,130 --> 00:09:48,630
经过所有这些工作，模型写下了这个词。
All that work and the model has written the.

104
00:09:49,678 --> 00:09:51,830
这就是一次推理步，也是整个模型的全部。
That's one trip and that's the whole model.

105
00:09:52,990 --> 00:09:59,844
其中的几乎每一步都是乘以权重，所以让我们降到最后一个层级，也就是其中任何一个权重。
Nearly every step of it was multiplying by the settings so come down one last level to a single one of them.

106
00:10:00,610 --> 00:10:02,130
这是模型的底层。
This is the floor of the model.

107
00:10:02,930 --> 00:10:11,190
如果给这个开放模型的每一个权重都分配一块指甲盖大小的瓷砖，把它们并排铺开，
这块马赛克将覆盖曼哈顿三分之二的面积。
Give every setting of that open model a tile the size of your fingernail, lay them side by side,
and the mosaic would cover two-thirds of Manhattan.

108
00:10:12,014 --> 00:10:13,930
我们现在正站在其中一块瓷砖上。
We are standing on one tile.

109
00:10:14,734 --> 00:10:19,730
它只有一种颜色，单凭它本身无法告诉你关于鸡蛋或任何其他事情的信息。
It has one color, and by itself it tells you nothing about eggs or anything else.

110
00:10:20,558 --> 00:10:27,250
模型似乎知道的一切，都包含在所有这些瓷砖共同构成的画面中，而且必须有人去设置每一块。
Whatever the model seems to know is in the picture that all those tiles make together, and somebody must have set every one of them.

111
00:10:29,750 --> 00:10:30,450
并没有人这样做。
Nobody did.

112
00:10:31,214 --> 00:10:36,260
要看它是如何做到的，我们得回到你的问题之前，到模型被制造的时候。
To see how, we have to go back to before your question, to when the model was being made.

113
00:10:37,390 --> 00:10:41,284
在第一天，它的权重是随机的，它写出的内容也是随机的。
On the first day, its settings are random, and so is everything it writes.

114
00:10:41,750 --> 00:10:43,830
问它关于鸡蛋的问题，你会得到一堆乱七八糟的东西。
Ask it about eggs, and you get a jumble.

115
00:10:44,654 --> 00:10:48,310
然后训练就开始了，这是一个不断重复的练习。
Then the training begins, and it is one exercise, repeated.

116
00:10:49,198 --> 00:10:55,730
模型被展示一段真实的文字，其中下一个词被遮住，比如，“将鸡蛋轻轻放入沸水”。
The model is shown a line of real writing with the next word covered, say, lower the egg gently into the boiling.

117
00:10:56,654 --> 00:11:00,810
它给出概率预测，而在这个阶段，“水”这个词几乎没有获得任何概率。
It gives its odds, and at this stage, water gets almost none.

118
00:11:01,614 --> 00:11:02,750
然后那个词被揭开。
Then the word is uncovered.

119
00:11:03,950 --> 00:11:14,020
现在，每一个权重都会微调一点点，朝着能让这一行
末尾更接近“水”的那个方向去，从而给它更高的概率。
Now, every setting is nudged, by a hair, in whichever direction would have
brought the end of the row a little nearer to water, and so given it better odds.

120
00:11:14,770 --> 00:11:21,410
找到那个方向是针对每个权重进行的额外计算，判断是稍微多一点还是少一点会有帮助。
Finding that direction is one more calculation, for each setting, whether a touch more would have helped, or a touch less.

121
00:11:22,190 --> 00:11:25,790
这个练习也是它之所以一次只回答你一个词的原因。
That exercise is also why it answers you one word at a time.

122
00:11:26,190 --> 00:11:29,750
这个练习唯一要求的只有下一个词。
The next word is the only thing this exercise ever asks for.

123
00:11:30,830 --> 00:11:31,850
那是绘制地图的人。
That's who drew the map.

124
00:11:31,918 --> 00:11:43,070
记住，地址包含在权重之中，所以它们和所有其他内容一起接受了微调；
而“鸡蛋”和“牛奶”出现在类似的句子中，同样的微调就把它们推向了一起。
The addresses are among the settings, remember, so they were nudged with all the rest,
and egg and milk turn up in the same kinds of sentence, so the same nudges push them together.

125
00:11:44,302 --> 00:11:52,350
这些数据主要来自公共互联网、食谱、新闻、争论、教科书和计算机程序。
The reading is drawn largely from the public internet, recipes, news, arguments, textbooks, computer programs.

126
00:11:53,070 --> 00:12:04,430
那个开放模型训练的数据量比一个人没日没夜阅读的还要多，它能处理成千上万年的内容，
而且有数千个芯片在并行处理这些数据。
That open model was trained on more of it than one person, reading day and night, It could get through in tens of thousands of years,
and thousands of chips worked through it side by side.

127
00:12:05,550 --> 00:12:07,630
它的制造者从未选择过单一的权重。
Its makers never chose a single setting.

128
00:12:08,462 --> 00:12:12,990
他们选择了阅读材料，并构建了执行微调的机制，而阅读内容完成了剩下的工作。
They chose the reading and built the thing that does the nudging, and the reading did the rest.

129
00:12:15,566 --> 00:12:22,240
那么，你是如何从那个毫无意义的底层瓦片，跨越到能告诉你关于鸡蛋知识的模型中的呢？
So how do you get from that floor, one tile that means nothing, up to a model that can tell you about eggs?

130
00:12:22,798 --> 00:12:27,840
到目前为止，它只被训练去预测下一个词，所以如果你觉得它像个鹦鹉，那是正确的。
So far, it has only been trained to guess the next word, and you'd be right to expect a parrot.

131
00:12:28,494 --> 00:12:31,720
但想想真正擅长预测需要具备什么条件。
But think about what it takes to be really good at guessing.

132
00:12:32,878 --> 00:12:33,900
有些词很容易猜。
Some words are easy.

133
00:12:34,660 --> 00:12:40,160
在“感谢”之后，你有十分中九次的把握，而次优选项是“感恩节”的其余部分。
After thank, you gets nine chances in ten, and the runner-up is the rest of Thanksgiving.

134
00:12:41,080 --> 00:12:41,980
这属于习惯。
That much is habit.

135
00:12:42,926 --> 00:12:48,680
然后它遇到像“把一个大鸡蛋煮大约”这样的句子，这时候习惯就没法完成预测了。
Then it meets a line like boil a large egg for about, and no habit will finish that.

136
00:12:49,454 --> 00:12:54,564
为了猜出这个词，模型需要事实，因此微调将事实压入权重中。
To guess it, the model needs the fact, so the nudging presses facts into the settings.

137
00:12:55,438 --> 00:13:03,000
在从未被注入事实的地方，轮盘仍有一个偏好，所以即便如
此，相关词汇也能蹦出来，而且听起来和其它词一样笃定。
Where a fact was never pressed in, the wheel still has a favorite, so a
word can come out anyway and it can sound just as sure as all the rest.

138
00:13:03,822 --> 00:13:06,060
这就是这些模型会胡编乱造的一个原因。
That's one reason these models can make things up.

139
00:13:07,150 --> 00:13:10,468
接着它遇到“36 加 59 等于……”
Then it meets 36 plus 59 is…

140
00:13:11,214 --> 00:13:16,960
这个它可能以前遇到过，但如果把数字变长，很快就会出现任何存储空间都无法容纳的计算量。
that one it might have met before, but make the numbers longer and there are soon more sums than anything could store.

141
00:13:17,774 --> 00:13:24,100
要停止在从未见过的加法中出错，唯一的办法就是在这
些权重中的某个地方，拥有某种能够进行加法的逻辑。
The only way to stop being wrong about sums it has never seen is to have, somewhere in those settings, something that adds.

142
00:13:24,878 --> 00:13:26,680
没人告诉模型去学习算术。
Nobody told the model to learn arithmetic.

143
00:13:27,630 --> 00:13:30,780
微调仅仅在说：词选错了，做得更好点。
The nudges only ever said, wrong word, do better.

144
00:13:31,534 --> 00:13:34,800
学会加法只是为了做得更好而剩下的唯一途径。
Learning to add was simply the only way left of doing better.

145
00:13:36,380 --> 00:13:44,480
OpenAI 的创始人之一、ChatGPT 背后的公司负责人伊利亚·苏茨克维尔，
用一个侦探故事解释了这其中的逻辑。
Ilya Sutskever, one of the founders of OpenAI, the company behind ChatGPT, explains how far this goes with a detective story.

146
00:13:45,230 --> 00:13:50,140
想象一个有十几个嫌疑人和 400 页线索的长篇悬案。
Picture a long mystery with a dozen suspects and 400 pages of clues.

147
00:13:50,140 --> 00:13:56,676
在最后一页，侦探把所有人聚集到起居室并说：凶手是……
On the last page, the detective gathers everyone in the drawing room and says, the murderer is...

148
00:13:57,166 --> 00:13:58,140
现在猜出下一个词。
now guess the next word.

149
00:13:58,894 --> 00:14:01,220
只有遵循了整个故事，你才能猜对。
You can only get it if you have followed the story.

150
00:14:02,446 --> 00:14:10,960
一个被推敲数万亿次去预测它所读内容中下一个词的模型，实际上是在被推动去遵循故事逻辑。
A model that is pushed trillions of times over to guess the next word of everything it reads is being pushed to follow the story.

151
00:14:11,380 --> 00:14:12,080
谁知道是什么？
Who knows what?

152
00:14:12,420 --> 00:14:13,560
什么是基于什么推导出来的？
What follows from what?

153
00:14:13,740 --> 00:14:14,840
证明的逻辑是怎样的？
How a proof goes?

154
00:14:14,840 --> 00:14:16,780
程序试图做什么。
what a program is trying to do.

155
00:14:17,260 --> 00:14:19,680
任何有助于它预测的内容都会被强化进去。
Whatever helps it guess gets pressed in.

156
00:14:20,718 --> 00:14:23,440
它不能通过复制来达到目的，因为空间不够。
It can't get there by copying because there isn't room.

157
00:14:24,160 --> 00:14:29,040
对于那个开放模型来说，这就像被要求把一整排书架塞进一本书里。
For that open model it's like being asked to fit a whole bookcase into a single book.

158
00:14:29,774 --> 00:14:36,540
它确实会背诵一些片段，但对于它读的大部分内容，只有共同点才能被保留下来。
It does learn some passages by heart, but for most of what it reads there is room only for what the books have in common.

159
00:14:36,910 --> 00:14:40,740
句子的结构、煮鸡蛋的方法、加法的原理。
How sentences go, how eggs are cooked, how sums work.

160
00:14:41,838 --> 00:14:45,900
至少那是理论，而且多年来一直没有好的方法去验证它。
That was the theory, at least, and for years there was no good way to check it.

161
00:14:46,638 --> 00:14:51,120
这些权重只是数字，你盯着它们看一整天也学不到任何东西。
The settings are only numbers, and you can stare at them all day without learning a thing.

162
00:14:53,710 --> 00:14:55,850
然后，开发者们开始寻找进入其中的办法。
Then the makers began to find a way in.

163
00:14:56,570 --> 00:15:02,630
在 Anthropic 公司（也就是 Claude 背后的公司），研
究人员为他们自己的模型构建了一个所谓的「显微镜」。
At Anthropic, the company behind Claude, researchers built what they call a microscope for their own models.

164
00:15:03,470 --> 00:15:04,970
它并不试图去读取这些权重数值。
It doesn't try to read the settings.

165
00:15:05,806 --> 00:15:12,150
它观察那些在模型运行过程中不断变化的行和列表，并寻找它们持续变化的方向。
It watches the row, the lists that shift while the model works, and looks for the directions they keep shifting in.

166
00:15:12,846 --> 00:15:17,210
在我们的地图上，从男人到女人的转变就是这样一个方向。
On our map, the step from man to woman was one such direction.

167
00:15:18,050 --> 00:15:23,070
在一个模型中，它发现了数百万个这样的方向，其中很多都是可以被命名的概念。
In one model, it found millions of them, and a great many are ideas you could put a name to.

168
00:15:24,014 --> 00:15:25,990
其中一个就是 Golden Gate Bridge。
One was the Golden Gate Bridge.

169
00:15:26,574 --> 00:15:33,490
当模型读到大桥的名字并看到照片时，这个概念就会
显现出来，所以它是一个概念，而不仅仅是一个词。
It showed up when the model read the bridge's name and when it was shown a photograph, so it is an idea and not just a word.

170
00:15:34,222 --> 00:15:42,470
可以确定的是，研究人员进入数据中手动强化了这个概念，之后模型就再也无法忽视这座大桥了。
To be sure, the researchers reached into the row and turned that one idea up by hand,
and the model could no longer leave the bridge alone.

171
00:15:43,090 --> 00:15:48,750
当被问到如何花掉 10 美元时，它建议开车穿过 Golden Gate Bridge 并支付过路费。
Asked how to spend $10, it suggested driving across the Golden Gate Bridge and paying the toll.

172
00:15:50,286 --> 00:15:53,610
他们的另一个模型展示了这些概念是如何在步骤中运作的。
Another of their models showed such ideas at work, in steps.

173
00:15:54,350 --> 00:15:59,310
询问达拉斯所在州的首府，通过显微镜你可以观察到它推导出的过程。
Ask for the capital of the state where Dallas is, and through the microscope you can watch it get there.

174
00:15:59,598 --> 00:16:02,830
首先是德克萨斯州，然后从德克萨斯州推导出奥斯汀。
First, Texas, and then, from Texas, Austin.

175
00:16:03,510 --> 00:16:08,350
当研究人员介入并将德克萨斯更换为加利福尼亚时，它给出的答案是萨克拉门托。
When the researchers reached in and swapped Texas for California, it answered Sacramento.

176
00:16:09,230 --> 00:16:12,550
这是一个正在推导出的答案，这比任何鹦鹉做的都要强。
That's an answer being worked out, which is more than any parrot does.

177
00:16:13,750 --> 00:16:16,750
接着，他们测试了一个最符合直觉的假设。
Then they tested the thing you would most expect to be true.

178
00:16:17,070 --> 00:16:21,670
即：一个一次只写一个词的模型不可能知道它的句子要走向何方。
That a model which writes one word at a time can't know where its sentence is going.

179
00:16:22,490 --> 00:16:24,170
他们给它一段押韵诗的第一行。
They gave it the first line of a rhyme.

180
00:16:24,590 --> 00:16:27,850
他看到了一根胡萝卜并不得不抓起它，然后要求给出第二行。
He saw a carrot and had to grab it, and asked for the second.

181
00:16:28,494 --> 00:16:32,950
他们原本预期只有在接近句子末尾时，它才会开始寻找押韵词。
They expected it to start hunting for a rhyme only when it got near the end of the line.

182
00:16:33,742 --> 00:16:36,650
这就是他们想要证明的，但他们错了。
That's what they had set out to show, and they were wrong.

183
00:16:37,774 --> 00:16:46,810
在它写出第二行第一个词之前，模型就已经选好了结
尾的那个词「兔子」，然后才写出通往那里的句子。
Before it had written a single word of the second line, the model had already
picked the word it would end on, rabbit, then it wrote a line that arrived there.

184
00:16:47,566 --> 00:16:52,370
当他们把「兔子」拿走时，它改用「习惯」作为句子的结尾。
When they reached in and took rabbit away, it ended the line on habit instead.

185
00:16:54,090 --> 00:16:56,570
在没有任何记录的情况下，「兔子」被保存在哪里？
Where was rabbit kept, with nothing written down?

186
00:16:57,326 --> 00:16:57,890
就在那排里。
In the row.

187
00:16:58,610 --> 00:17:10,210
它的列表包含的内容远比我们看到的那个词要多，而这些内容在推理步与推理步之间，
都被保存在我们第一部影片中提到的记忆塔中，在那里，你的问题以电荷模式的形式等待着。
Its lists hold far more than the one word we get to see, and what they hold is kept from trip to trip in the towers of memory from our
first film, where your question waits as a pattern of electric charges.

188
00:17:10,868 --> 00:17:14,368
每一个后续的词都可以回溯并找到那里的「兔子」。
Each later word could look back and find rabbit there.

189
00:17:15,270 --> 00:17:19,670
所以这个只选择下一个词的模型，其实先选定了目的地。
So the model that only ever chooses the next word had chosen its destination first.

190
00:17:20,462 --> 00:17:22,710
一个可能的原因又是侦探故事。
A likely reason is the detective story again.

191
00:17:23,210 --> 00:17:27,329
除非你知道句子最终要落在哪，否则很难猜出押韵句的中间部分。
It's hard to guess the middle of a rhyming line unless you know where the line has to land.

192
00:17:28,450 --> 00:17:31,190
提醒一下，其中大部分是研究人员拥有的。
A word of caution, most of it the researchers own.

193
00:17:31,982 --> 00:17:33,150
这些只是简短的例子。
These were short examples.

194
00:17:33,150 --> 00:17:38,690
在那些不是开发者最大规模的模型中，显微镜只能看到发生过程的一部分。
in models that were not their maker's largest and the microscope shows only a part of what goes on.

195
00:17:41,210 --> 00:17:46,950
达拉斯、德克萨斯、奥斯汀，这是在单次推理步中跨出的两步。
Dallas, Texas, Austin, that was two steps taken inside a single trip.

196
00:17:47,694 --> 00:17:52,650
现在再往上提高一个层级，给同一个模型一些比煮鸡蛋难得多的任务。
Now go one level higher and give the same model something much harder than an egg.

197
00:17:53,678 --> 00:17:56,930
从你自己开始，还有 23 乘以 47。
Start with yourself and with 23 times 47.

198
00:17:57,774 --> 00:18:02,030
我们大多数人无法在脑子里一次性算出来，但只要给我们一支铅笔，就很容易了。
Most of us can't do that in our heads in one go, but hand us a pencil and it's easy.

199
00:18:02,790 --> 00:18:11,070
23 乘以 40 是 920，23 乘以 7 是 161，两者相加就是 1081。
23 times 40 is 920, 23 times 7 is 161, and the two together make 1,081.

200
00:18:11,470 --> 00:18:14,370
用三个简单的步骤代替一个复杂的步骤。
Three easy steps in place of one hard one.

201
00:18:15,502 --> 00:18:17,410
模型也有类似的限制。
The model has a limit of the same kind.

202
00:18:18,170 --> 00:18:27,210
一次推理步是固定数量的轮次，不会再多一点，所以如果一个问题需要比这更多的步骤，
无论权重设置得多么好，它都无法容纳在单次推理步中。
One trip is a fixed number of rounds and not one more, so a problem that needs more
steps than that will not fit into a single trip, however good the settings are.

203
00:18:28,334 --> 00:18:29,910
但模型内置了「草稿纸」。
But the model has paper built in.

204
00:18:30,574 --> 00:18:38,890
它写下的每一个词都会回到那排里，并在下一次推理步中被再次读取，这样它就能写下一个步骤，
阅读自己的步骤，然后进行下一步。
Every word it writes goes back into the row and is read again on the next
trip, so it can write a step down, read its own step, and take the next one.

205
00:18:39,430 --> 00:18:43,470
一旦前一个步骤出现在页面上，每一个步骤就变成了一个简单的预测。
Each step is an easy guess once the step before it is on the page.

206
00:18:45,102 --> 00:18:46,530
这就是所谓的「思考」。
That's all that thinking is.

207
00:18:47,310 --> 00:18:59,110
当你向大型模型询问难题时，它告诉你它在思考，实际上它是在给自己写笔记，
这些笔记你通常看不到，或者只能看到摘要，而这一切都在它写出回答的第一个词之前就完成了。
When you ask one of the big models something hard and it tells you it's thinking, it is writing notes to itself,
which you often don't see, or see only in summary, all before it writes the first word of your answer.

208
00:18:59,110 --> 00:19:02,750
这是同一种模型的变体，只是它进行了更多的推理步。
It is the same kind of model, making many more trips.

209
00:19:03,918 --> 00:19:14,810
这些笔记中的每一个词都意味着多一次通过权重的推理步，以及更多次的乘法运算，
这就是为什么难题可能需要一分钟，而煮鸡蛋只需要几秒钟。
Every word of those notes is one more trip through the settings, and one more helping of all that multiplying,
which is why a hard question can take a minute where the egg took a few seconds.

210
00:19:17,454 --> 00:19:23,840
一个难题变成了长长的微小步骤链，那么问题就在于知道该选哪些步骤。
A hard problem becomes a long chain of small steps, then, and the trouble is knowing which steps.

211
00:19:24,622 --> 00:19:31,280
在证明的每一行中，循环都会重新开始，而在它可能写出的上千个词中，几乎所有都毫无意义。
At every line of a proof, the wheel is back, and of the thousand things it could write next, nearly all lead nowhere.

212
00:19:32,110 --> 00:19:39,200
你可能会读过所有出版过的数学书籍，但依然不知道该尝试哪一个，
任何参加过考试的人都能告诉你这一点。
You could read every mathematics book ever printed and still not know which to try, as anyone who has taken an exam will tell you.

213
00:19:39,918 --> 00:19:42,880
这需要通过有人检查你的推导过程来练习。
For that you need practice, with somebody checking your work.

214
00:19:43,950 --> 00:19:46,060
这就是为什么大型模型要进行两次“上学”。
That's why the big models go to school twice.

215
00:19:46,800 --> 00:19:50,640
在大量阅读之后，它们还会通过试错与奖励进行再次训练。
After all the reading, they are trained again, by trial and reward.

216
00:19:51,260 --> 00:19:55,560
这被称为强化学习，有一家实验室公布了具体方法。
It's called reinforcement learning, and one laboratory has published how.

217
00:19:56,046 --> 00:20:04,080
模型被赋予一些答案可查验的问题，比如有已知答案的算术题，或者能正常运行或报错的程序。
The model is set problems whose answers can be checked, a sum with a known answer, or a program that either runs properly or doesn't.

218
00:20:04,720 --> 00:20:09,020
因为每个词都是随机抽取的，所以每次尝试的结果都略有不同。
Because every word is drawn by chance, each attempt comes out a little different.

219
00:20:09,700 --> 00:20:18,000
当结果正确时，权重就会微调，让下一次“轮盘”更倾向于那个方向；如果错误，则减少一点。
When one comes out right, the settings are nudged so that the wheel leans
that way a little more next time, and when it comes out wrong, a little less.

220
00:20:18,862 --> 00:20:21,020
这些课程从没教过它该如何去做。
That lesson never says how to do it.

221
00:20:21,742 --> 00:20:27,360
模型被要求在给出答案前写下推导过程，并且当答案正确时会获得奖励。
The model was told only to write its working down before its answer, and it was rewarded when the answer was right.

222
00:20:27,360 --> 00:20:33,820
然而，它越来越多地开始检查自己的工作，并放弃那些毫无进展的方法去尝试另一种方法。
Yet, more and more, it checked its own work and dropped an approach that was getting nowhere to try another.

223
00:20:34,606 --> 00:20:38,468
它读到过人们这样做，而实践证明了这些做法是有效的。
It had read about people doing those things, and practice showed it that they worked.

224
00:20:40,078 --> 00:20:43,700
在处理程序时，它往往拥有每个程序员都拥有的优势。
With programs, it often has the advantage every programmer has.

225
00:20:44,080 --> 00:20:46,468
它可以运行自己写出的内容并查看结果。
It can run what it has written and look.

226
00:20:47,214 --> 00:20:53,560
报错信息以文字形式返回，与其他内容一样加入到序列中，下一个词就会考虑到这些信息。
The error message comes back as words, joins the row like everything else, and the next guest takes it into account.

227
00:20:54,542 --> 00:21:05,900
2025年7月，Google DeepMind 的一个 Gemini 版本被要求做 International Mathematical
Olympiad 的题目，这是全球顶尖的中学生数学竞赛。
In July 2025, a version of Gemini, from Google DeepMind, was set the papers of the International Mathematical Olympiad,
the world's top mathematics contest for school students.

228
00:21:06,440 --> 00:21:13,120
这些题目每年都会重新编写并保密直到比赛当天，所以答案并不在模型读过的任何资料中。
Its problems are written new each year and kept secret until the day, so the answers were in nothing the model had read.

229
00:21:13,870 --> 00:21:18,680
和学生一样，它接受过辅导，并且拥有同样的试卷和同样九小时的时间。
Like the students, it had been coached, and it had the same papers and the same nine hours.

230
00:21:18,680 --> 00:21:24,680
与学生不同的是，它可以同时运行多次尝试，并解决了六道题中的五道。
Unlike the students, it could run many attempts side-by-side, and it solved five of the six.

231
00:21:25,300 --> 00:21:32,340
奥赛官方的阅卷人给它的作品评分，结果是金牌水平，大约只有九分之一的学生能拿到这个成绩。
The Olympiad's own graders marked its work, and it was a gold medal score, which about one student in nine takes home.

232
00:21:33,820 --> 00:21:44,600
同年9月，在大学编程竞赛的世界总决赛中，一个 Gemini 版本解决了12道题中的10道，
其中还有一道是没有任何人类团队能解出的。
That September, at the World Finals of the University Programming Contest, a version
of Gemini solved ten of the twelve problems, among them one that no human team solved.

233
00:21:45,422 --> 00:21:47,280
最强的参赛人类团队解出了11道。
The best human team solved eleven.

234
00:21:48,270 --> 00:21:51,740
它读到目前为止的所有内容，然后再写下一个词。
It reads everything so far, and it writes one more word.

235
00:21:52,500 --> 00:21:55,220
这和之前的“煮鸡蛋”例子是一样的，只不过过程要长得多。
It's the egg all over again, only a great deal longer.

236
00:21:57,830 --> 00:22:04,870
这类模型能获得数学金牌水平，所以你可能会预期它能告诉你它是如何把两个小数字相加的。
Models of this kind can earn a gold medal score in mathematics, so you
would expect one to be able to tell you how it adds two small numbers.

237
00:22:06,094 --> 00:22:12,830
Anthropic 的研究人员给他们的一个较小模型一个算术题：36 加 59，并观察它的推导过程。
The researchers at Anthropic gave one of their smaller models a sum, 36 plus 59, and watched it work.

238
00:22:13,582 --> 00:22:17,050
它并没有用你学过的方法去计算，也没有按照要求的步骤去做。
It didn't do the sum the way you were taught, and nothing it asked it to.

239
00:22:17,614 --> 00:22:20,930
权重微调只标记了答案，从未标注过方法。
The nudges only ever marked the answer, never the method.

240
00:22:21,742 --> 00:22:34,890
它并列运行了两行推导过程：其中一行比较粗略，只说结果在90左右；另一行则只关心末位，
即6和9相加会得到以5结尾的数。
It ran two lines of working side by side, one of them rough, saying only that the answer is somewhere around 90,
and the other caring about nothing but the last digit, where a 6 and a 9 make something that ends in 5.

241
00:22:35,758 --> 00:22:37,590
把这两者结合起来，就得到了95。
Put the two together and you have 95.

242
00:22:39,022 --> 00:22:41,030
然后他们询问模型它是如何完成计算的。
Then they asked the model how it had done it.

243
00:22:41,742 --> 00:22:47,490
它说它把6和9相加，进位1，然后把十位相加。
It said that it had added the 6 and the 9, carried the 1, and then added the 10s.

244
00:22:48,238 --> 00:22:51,190
那是学校里教的方法，并不是它实际操作的方式。
That's the method from school, and it is not what it did.

245
00:22:51,830 --> 00:22:57,830
它大概只是在预测下一个词，而那种方法是人类解释加法时的套路。
Most likely its account was one more guess at the next word, and that method is how people explain a sum.

246
00:22:59,310 --> 00:23:02,870
所以它可以按自己的方式做加法，但它没法告诉你具体是怎么做的。
So it can add after its fashion, and it can't tell you how.

247
00:23:03,598 --> 00:23:04,410
它并不是在撒谎。
It isn't lying.

248
00:23:04,930 --> 00:23:10,590
起码在这里，它对自身推导过程的认知，并不比你对脑细胞的了解更清晰。
Here, at least, it had no more view of its own working than you have of your own brain cells.

249
00:23:11,342 --> 00:23:16,630
它的开发者警告说，它在思考时写下的笔记并不总是反映其内部真实情况。
Its makers warn that the notes it writes while thinking are not always what went on inside.

250
00:23:19,278 --> 00:23:20,800
所以让我们继续深入探究下去。
So let's climb the rest of the way out.

251
00:23:21,870 --> 00:23:24,740
人工智能是怎么知道煮鸡蛋要花多长时间的？
How does an AI know how long to boil an egg?

252
00:23:25,454 --> 00:23:26,700
没人把它写进程序里的。
Nobody programmed it in.

253
00:23:27,374 --> 00:23:38,280
它阅读了人类写下的内容，预测每一个后续词汇，并在每次预测后不断修正，
直到这种成功的预测在它的权重中形成了一种非常像「知晓」的机制。
It read what people had written, guessing every next word and being nudged after every guess until guessing well had pressed into its
settings something that works very much like knowing.

254
00:23:38,280 --> 00:23:44,484
接着它进行了练习，并经过校验，直到它能将微小的步骤串联成长链。
Then it practiced, with its work checked, until it could string small steps into long chains.

255
00:23:45,678 --> 00:23:48,480
这类模型内部的每一次计算都是可知的。
Every calculation inside a model like this is known.

256
00:23:49,240 --> 00:23:53,120
目前还做不到的是去观察这些权重并读懂它们的含义。
What can't yet be done is to look at the settings and read what they mean.

257
00:23:53,902 --> 00:24:03,920
显微镜式研究已经迈出了第一步，但在2025年，这家公司的负责人写道：
「我们并不理解我们创造的人工智能是如何运作的。」该公司的创始人之一喜欢说，
The microscope has made a start, but in 2025, the head of the company that built it wrote,
''We do not understand how our own AI creations work.'' One of that company's

258
00:24:03,920 --> 00:24:04,200
显微镜式研究已经迈出了第一步，但在2025年，这家公司的负责人写道：
「我们并不理解我们创造的人工智能是如何运作的。」该公司的创始人之一喜欢说，
founders likes to say that these AI systems are grown more than they are built.

259
00:24:04,200 --> 00:24:08,680
这些人工智能系统更多是「生长」出来的，而非被「制造」出来的。
founders likes to say that these AI systems are grown more than they are built.

260
00:24:09,774 --> 00:24:16,080
所以下次当答案在你面前一个词接一个词地蹦出来时，你就会明白你在看什么了。
So the next time an answer types itself out in front of you, one word after another, you'll know what you are watching.

261
00:24:16,780 --> 00:24:27,080
在某个地方，一个由开关组成的星系正在数以十亿计的权重中进行乘法运算，
而这些权重并非由人设置，它们正通往它可能已经选定的那个词。
Somewhere, a galaxy of switches is multiplying its way through billions upon billions of settings that nobody set,
on its way to a word it may already have chosen.

262
00:24:27,918 --> 00:24:30,200
而你最初问的只是：煮个鸡蛋要多久？
And all you asked was how long to boil an egg.

